 concerning the linkages between the EU Green Deal and the 8th Environmental Action Plan; evaluation of the existing EU Biodiversity Strategy; the progress with the implementation of the EU Action Plan for nature, people and the economy; about the future of CAP; restoration targets in the new strategy, etc. The EHF has also made a few concrete proposals for targets that should be considered when developing the new EU Biodiversity Strategy to 2030:

  • the new Target 1 to state that by 2030, all species and habitats are in favourable or improving conservation status; by 2050 all species and habitats are in favourable conservation status (FCS);
  • EHF supports the target of 30% target of marine and terrestrial protected areas by 2030 (that can include nationally designated PAs) but these areas also need to be well managed;
  • There should be a reduction of pesticides at the farm level, with a full nutrient balance at a catchment-basin level by 2030, and there should be obligatory 10% of farm-level green infrastructure;
  • EHF strongly advocates that by 2030 there is a legally binding large-scale restoration initiative focusing on ecosystems that are important for biodiversity and climate change mitigation and adaptation (such as old-growth forests, grasslands, heathlands, and wetlands).

The general discussion led to the conclusion that all quantitative indicators should be based on thorough scientific data and that these should be accompanied by qualitative indicators (e.g. referring to the quality of habitats or effective management measures). 

Eurosite is determined to closely follow the development of the new EU Biodiversity Strategy, particularly in the fields that are most important for our members, such as management of nature conservation sites, habitat maintenance, restoration and monitoring and securing the appropriate funding opportunities for effective site conservation and management.

